      "vulnerable_function": "getSwapFee",
      "location": "CLFactory.getSwapFee",
      "explanation": "CLFactory integrates an external IFeeModule to fetch a dynamic swap fee, but it hard-caps the accepted module-returned fee at 100_000 ppm (10%). If the module returns any fee > 100_000 (e.g., 200_000 = 20%), the factory silently discards it and falls back to the tickSpacingToFee default (often ~500 ppm = 0.05%). This creates a dangerous mismatch: governance/operators can configure a higher fee in the module (the module may allow up to 500_000 = 50%), see that configuration off-chain, and believe the high fee is active, while on-chain enforcement remains at the low default. This is exploitable because any trader (including MEV bots) benefits from paying far less than intended during periods when a high fee is supposed to deter abusive trading or increase revenue.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Governance configures the swapFeeModule to return 200_000 ppm (20%) for a specific pool (e.g., during launch/anti-MEV period).\n2) swapFeeModule.getFee(pool) correctly returns 200_000.\n3) Any trader/MEV bot calls into the pool’s swap path that relies on factory.getSwapFee(pool).\n4) CLFactory.getSwapFee() sees fee=200_000 (>100_000) and ignores it, returning tickSpacingToFee[pool.tickSpacing()] (e.g., 500 ppm).\n5) The attacker executes high-volume/sandwich/arbitrage trades paying ~0.05% instead of 20%, defeating the intended protection and reducing protocol fee revenue.",
      "suggested_fix": "Make the fee cap consistent and non-silent:\n- Option A (safer): revert if module returns an out-of-range fee (e.g., >100_000), so misconfiguration is immediately visible.\n- Option B: raise the factory cap to match the module’s supported maximum (e.g., 500_000).\n- Additionally, emit an event or add explicit validation tooling when setting modules/config so out-of-range configurations cannot persist unnoticed."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "The contract’s primary security issue is a logic mismatch in dynamic swap fee enforcement: fees above 10% returned by the external fee module are silently ignored and replaced with the tick-spacing default. This enables any trader to pay much lower fees than governance intends, undermining anti-MEV/protective measures and reducing revenue, with no on-chain alert indicating the misconfiguration."
}
